// contrib/http/rescue.mere -- one bad route must not take the server with it.
//
// WHAT IT WAS. A handler that fails -- `read_file` on a path that is not there,
// `of_json` on input it refuses, an index out of range -- unwinds past the
// server loop and ends the PROCESS. Measured, on the readiness server with four
// workers: GET /ok answers, GET /boom is a failing route, and the next GET /ok
// cannot connect because nothing is listening any more. One request path takes
// the whole site down, and it is the path nobody tested.
//
// That is not a property of the server shape. `http_serve`, `http_serve_mt` and
// `http_serve_rd` all behave this way, because none of them was catching
// anything: the language HAS `try_or`, and contrib/http simply never used it.
//
//   import "http/rescue.mere";
//   let _ = http_serve_rd 8080 8 (with_rescue (router routes not_found));
//
// WHY `Some`/`None` RATHER THAN A DEFAULT BODY. `try_or thunk default` takes a
// VALUE, not a second thunk, so the default is evaluated whether or not the
// thunk fails. A default of `let _ = http_set_status 500 in "..."` would set 500
// on EVERY request, including the ones that succeed. Wrapping the result in an
// option keeps the default pure -- `None` -- and puts the status change in the
// branch that is only taken on failure.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT CATCH, said plainly: `try_or` catches a Mere `fail`. A stack
// overflow, a segfault in an extern, or a worker killed by a signal are not
// that, and they still end the process. What this buys is that a bug in one
// handler stops being an outage.
//
// THE RESPONSE BODY SAYS NOTHING. A failure message can carry a path, a query,
// or a row -- so it goes to stderr, where the operator reads it, and the client
// gets five words.

extern fn http_set_status: int -> unit;

let with_rescue = fn (inner) -> fn (req: str) ->
  match try_or (fn () -> Some (inner req)) None with
  | Some body -> body
  | None ->
    let _ = print_err ("http: handler failed for " ++ req) in
    let _ = http_set_status 500 in
    "internal server error\n"
  ;

// The same, for a handler that takes per-worker context
// (http_serve_mt_ctx / http_serve_rd_ctx).
let with_rescue_ctx = fn (inner) -> fn (ctx: int) -> fn (req: str) ->
  match try_or (fn () -> Some (inner ctx req)) None with
  | Some body -> body
  | None ->
    let _ = print_err ("http: handler failed for " ++ req) in
    let _ = http_set_status 500 in
    "internal server error\n"
  ;
